[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause confirmed.** Look at router6's FORWARD chain — rules 2, 3, and 4 are DROP rules matching **all traffic arriving inbound on eth2** (the uplink to router3/the WAN):

```
Rule 2:  DROP  TCP  in=eth2  any→any
Rule 3:  DROP  UDP  in=eth2  any→any
Rule 4:  DROP  ALL  in=eth2  any→any   ← 3 pkts matched = the 3 ICMP replies from my PC11 test
```

Rule 1 is a narrow legacy ACCEPT (TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1), then rules 2–4 silently drop everything else arriving from the uplink. PC15's outbound ICMP echo *leaves* via eth2 unimpeded (no outbound block), reaches PC11/PC4 fine, but the **echo replies arrive back on eth2 and are dropped by rule 4**. IP forwarding is enabled (=1) so that's not the cause.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Location B's uplink router (router6) has three overly-broad DROP rules injected into its iptables FORWARD chain that match all traffic arriving inbound on eth2 — the interface connecting router6 to router3 and the rest of the network. Specifically, rule 2 drops all TCP, rule 3 drops all UDP, and rule 4 drops all protocols (proto=0 = "all") that arrive on eth2. A narrow ACCEPT ahead of them (rule 1) only passes TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to a single host.

The mechanism is asymmetric: outbound traffic from Location B (PC15-18, Laptop4/5) leaves router6 via eth2 toward router3 without any FORWARD-chain restriction, so echo requests do reach Location A and Location C. However, the return echo replies (and any other responses) arrive back on eth2 and are caught by rule 4, which matches every protocol and drops the packets before they can be forwarded to the 172.18.50.0/24 or 172.18.51.0/24 LAN interfaces. The counter on rule 4 already showed 3 dropped packets — exactly matching the three-packet ICMP test against PC11. Internal Location B traffic never traverses eth2 (it stays within 172.18.50/51 subnets directly attached to router6), so it is unaffected.

The root cause is that iptables rules 2, 3, and 4 on router6's FORWARD chain were added to drop all inbound eth2 traffic, breaking the return path for both Location A and Location C simultaneously.

Corrective fix: on router6, delete FORWARD rules 4, 3, and 2 in descending order (to avoid renumbering): `iptables -D FORWARD 4; iptables -D FORWARD 3; iptables -D FORWARD 2`. This restores the intended forwarding behaviour while leaving the narrow legacy ACCEPT (rule 1) in place.
=== END ===